{"title":"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solutions</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Construct_digits_of_exemplar.)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Construct digits of exemplar.)</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(<i>Titu Andreescu</i>)\nProve that for every positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> there exists an <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-digit number divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b216498b4cb64f29f3bfdfa60736dc769f06c7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> all of whose digits are odd.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solutions\">Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We proceed by induction.  For our base case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/9/1d96ecc15129c5631ba4a2c6578e91e3d13e3baf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, we have the number 5.  Now, suppose that there exists some number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/1/591b85fd717e9d391784416055555c11afa9fee1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a \\cdot 5^{n-1}$\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdca93962a2beaffc172ca394e04fe05bd74ffd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> digits, all of which are odd.  It is then sufficient to prove that there exists an odd digit <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/6/a16c3ba4eeb77096f97771650369cbd22e0e0281.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k\\cdot 10^{n-1} + a \\cdot 5^{n-1} = 5^{n-1}(k \\cdot 2^{n-1} + a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"313\" height=\"21\" /> is divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b216498b4cb64f29f3bfdfa60736dc769f06c7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />.  This is equivalent to proving that there exists an odd digit <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/1/4c10a3cd75271a628d876c7ac85fc971b5f5f3ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\cdot 2^{n-1} + a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"90\" height=\"16\" /> is divisible by 5, which is true when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/9/c5976538cc2bbbcba673b896ced7c95e6670f399.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\equiv -3^{n-1}a \\pmod{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"163\" height=\"19\" />.  Since there is an odd digit in each of the residue classes mod 5, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> exists and the induction is complete.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>First, we note that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b216498b4cb64f29f3bfdfa60736dc769f06c7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> digit numbers with only odd digits.  Now, we will prove that none of these numbers have the same residue mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b216498b4cb64f29f3bfdfa60736dc769f06c7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and therefore one of them must be 0 mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b216498b4cb64f29f3bfdfa60736dc769f06c7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><br />\nProof by contradiction:  \nAssume we have two distinct numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/7/1e77a489dab27abcb9cbaaf293eb8f98c03c42e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1A_2A_3...A_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"100\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/e/2dea866853d120711372dcdd74f3816c7744c61a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_1B_2B_3...B_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"100\" height=\"15\" /> with only odd digits that leave the same residue mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b216498b4cb64f29f3bfdfa60736dc769f06c7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />.  Then, subtracting the larger from the smaller would yield a new number that is a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b216498b4cb64f29f3bfdfa60736dc769f06c7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> and has only even digits.  We could then halve all of the digits in that number to get a second multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b216498b4cb64f29f3bfdfa60736dc769f06c7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> with at most n digits that only uses the digits 0 through 4.  \n</p><p><br />\nLemma: Every multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b216498b4cb64f29f3bfdfa60736dc769f06c7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> with n digits or less has a 5 as one of its digits.  \n</p><p>All numbers of this type can be written as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/c/c0c4302a35222243d166950bbc5eb7567849da22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k5^n$\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" />.  Then, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in it.  (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/b/99bbc17173c6e247a5130541a5aad46c92c0d253.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&lt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"45\" height=\"10\" />, or else our number would have more than n digits).  So, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/f/3bfb24e397d157cd0b1a094c14d7ad29c33be9b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k5^n=a2^x5^n=a*10^x*5^{n-x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"227\" height=\"13\" /> for some odd a.  Now <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/9/bc9e902ee872c92d719f4aec233bb3d2260a5d5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a*10^x*5^{n-x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"105\" height=\"13\" /> is an odd multiple of 5  (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/b/66b6e8aa2e57dcc8ca7673671daeaacdbfce9fba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a5^{n-x}$\" width=\"44\" height=\"12\" />)  with x zeroes after it, and all multiples of 5 end in 5.  Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/9/bc9e902ee872c92d719f4aec233bb3d2260a5d5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a*10^x*5^{n-x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"105\" height=\"13\" /> always contains a 5 as its <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/1/091bf4c63379d528c96b8b8e3e1a2fff654ba856.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x+1)^{st}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"19\" /> digit, and we have proven our lemma.  \n</p><p>By our lemma, our number with only the digits 0 through 4 cannot be a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/2/5b216498b4cb64f29f3bfdfa60736dc769f06c7d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, and so we have reached a contradiction.  QED\n</p><p>Note:  Not only does this prove the desired claim that there exists such a number, but it also proves that there is exactly one such number.\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Construct_digits_of_exemplar..29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Construct_digits_of_exemplar.)\">Solution 3 (Construct digits of exemplar.)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Construct digits of exemplar.)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/0/d6005cf6b89ee0a4eb308e8f4081e3dfd5a66e66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^n \\pmod {10} \\equiv 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"135\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/e/6fe43f825346bc3fe052739803d5e7cb96762315.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^k 5^n \\pmod {10^{k +1} } \\equiv  10^k 5^{n-k} \\equiv 5 \\cdot 10^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"303\" height=\"22\" />.\n</p><p>For any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, we can construct a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/c/accbf1a7904f2a00ab2c4bedae62ce78e4cddc2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 5^n \\cdot \\sum_{0 \\leq i &lt; n}{2^{x_i}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -25px\" width=\"133\" height=\"45\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/b/fcb3fc2febb1988b36512463c821633812603961.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_0 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/b/47b30ad1b871197a6b08106bc94ec92070eb3d4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i = 5^n \\sum_{0 \\leq j \\leq i}{2^{x_j}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -25px\" width=\"127\" height=\"45\" /> \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/c/19cad41b90df6ca49b846f1b7802fc2be256c690.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_{i} = \\frac{a_{i-1}}{10^{i}} + 1 \\pmod 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"182\" height=\"34\" />. (This makes the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/3/a03b2d3366c42d856af81cba08514c63164ff74c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10^{i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"26\" height=\"17\" /> place of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/7/2a772bd794fbedb2976268c491686644aefc02e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> odd, without changing the the smaller place digits).\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/d/15d1dd345ddd7573ba2019003225f15569b45ccd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = a_{n-1} = 5^n \\cdot  \\sum_{0 \\leq j &lt; n}{2^{x_j}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -25px\" width=\"195\" height=\"45\" /> ,  with digits in place <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/6/8a605afcc5b95a9a80915af1218cd379415674e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10^i$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"22\" height=\"15\" /> odd, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/6/9d691b06fc795e0b33c559f1c0544767eda96b77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\leq i&lt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/7/5d7b52b2185c60313d9b29a71f0a7e0d6efbee10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{0 \\leq j &lt; n}{2^{x_j}}  &lt; 2^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -25px\" width=\"113\" height=\"45\" />,  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/c/03c5b2e86b4b9faa5e46d765685c6d725866e8fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a &lt; 5^n 2^n = 10^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"123\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>By construction, the digits in all the places up through  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/d/c1d84fdd8a443af3b729ea401dc0f70d6ebf6115.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> are odd, and since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/9/189789c1eb69cd611a803ac5cfbc1132b21a2838.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&lt;10^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"63\" height=\"15\" />, there are no other digits. \n</p><p>In fact, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/1/5914a3122e66ebb628f222da0cb930f727bb080d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_m = a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"17\" /> that solves the case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/e/54e7191bf118e3cb1338a8307f3665f2f1299dca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"10\" />,  then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/1/a317cd303b910df77d2e248b8078bed7471106f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{m+1} \\mod 10^ m \\equiv A_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"179\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Note that in some cases (like <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/e/84e75ddd39b9408f4d8e7fa62d2f6cdca00232de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />) , both of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/3/2e38ba9b9cc96115165d32733331542663df712e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_{n-1} \\in \\{0,1\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"105\" height=\"20\" /> yield distinct numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6ef10e2548ed97eefe6001538077e6ecb4498b40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a-5\\cdot10^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"100\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> with all odd digits. \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6ef10e2548ed97eefe6001538077e6ecb4498b40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a-5\\cdot10^{n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"100\" height=\"18\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/1/751d8a6629b6c6dbb0542624cec3bc8e1a94eada.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n-1&lt;n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"79\" height=\"15\" /> digits, and so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/c/28cfda1d5d460d983b6582f0f4377cdd326e676f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_{n-1}=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"72\" height=\"18\" /> is needed for padding. \n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO\" title=\"2003 USAMO\">2003 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2003\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b>First question</b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2003 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solution